Title:
ExecuteAssemblyPICO

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
ExecuteAssemblyPICO is a Position Independent Code (PIC) implementation of an “execute-assembly” capability for running .NET assemblies from an in-memory runner. It uses the Crystal Palace linker to produce PIC-friendly artifacts suitable for use as shellcode/BOF-like payloads or custom loaders. The loader redirects the current process stdout to a named pipe to capture output from the executed .NET assembly, then loads a PICO “.NET assembly runner” and calls an exported `ExecuteAssembly` function with the target assembly bytes and arguments. This addresses the need to execute common offensive .NET tooling (e.g., Rubeus) without dropping an EXE to disk and with more flexible integration into implants/loaders. It’s primarily useful for red teams, pentesters, and implant developers building custom tradecraft around in-memory .NET execution.

Technical Focus:
- Position Independent Code (PIC) payload construction (Crystal Palace linker)
- In-memory .NET assembly execution (“execute-assembly” pattern)
- Named pipe stdout redirection/capture
- Custom loader + exported function interface (`ExecuteAssembly`)
- Integration patterns for offensive .NET tooling execution

Use Cases:
- Execute .NET post-exploitation tools (e.g., Rubeus) from a custom loader without writing to disk
- Embed a .NET runner as PIC into an implant for on-demand assembly execution
- Capture tool output reliably via named pipes for C2 tasking/response
- Build/experiment with PIC-based execution primitives for evasive tradecraft

Keywords:
execute-assembly, PIC, Position Independent Code, Crystal Palace linker, in-memory execution, .NET assembly runner, ExecuteAssembly export, named pipe, stdout redirection, loader, Rubeus, post-exploitation, shellcode-like payload, Tradecraft Garden, inject-assembly, offensive tooling integration